  "assets": [
    {
      "assetId": "math.prob.law-of-unconscious/learning_objective/1",
      "kind": "learning_objective",
      "conceptId": "math.prob.law-of-unconscious",
      "title": "Learning Objective",
      "content": "The student states the Law of the Unconscious Statistician (LOTUS): for a random variable X with PMF/PDF f_X, and any function g, E[g(X)]=∑ₓ g(x)f_X(x) (discrete) or ∫g(x)f_X(x)dx (continuous); applies LOTUS to compute E[X²], E[1/X], E[eˣ], and E[g(X)] for standard distributions without first finding the distribution of g(X); and explains why LOTUS is \"unconscious\" — it avoids computing the distribution of Y=g(X).",
      "localKey": null,
      "language": "en",
      "refs": [],
      "span": {
        "file": "docs/curriculum/blueprints/math.prob.law-of-unconscious.md",
        "startLine": 18,
        "endLine": 21
      }
    },
    {
      "assetId": "math.prob.law-of-unconscious/misconception/MC-1",
      "kind": "misconception",
      "conceptId": "math.prob.law-of-unconscious",
      "title": "E[g(X)]=g(E[X])",
      "content": "| MC-1 | E[g(X)]=g(E[X]) | Student computes E[X²]=(E[X])², E[eˣ]=e^{E[X]}, E[1/X]=1/E[X]; conflates expectation with direct function evaluation | Type 1 — overgeneralisation (E[aX+b]=aE[X]+b IS correct; students extend linearity to nonlinear functions) |",
      "localKey": "MC-1",
      "language": "en",
      "refs": [
        "MC-1"
      ],
      "span": {
        "file": "docs/curriculum/blueprints/math.prob.law-of-unconscious.md",
        "startLine": 28,
        "endLine": 28
      }
    },
    {
      "assetId": "math.prob.law-of-unconscious/misconception/MC-2",
      "kind": "misconception",
      "conceptId": "math.prob.law-of-unconscious",
      "title": "LOTUS-REQUIRES-KNOWING-DISTRIBUTION-OF-Y",
      "content": "| MC-2 | LOTUS-REQUIRES-KNOWING-DISTRIBUTION-OF-Y | Student thinks they must compute f_Y (the distribution of Y=g(X)) before using LOTUS; doesn't understand that LOTUS avoids this step | Type 5 — instruction-induced (the \"proper\" derivation of E[Y] for Y=g(X) first finds f_Y using the change-of-variable theorem; students who learn that method first assume it's always required) |",
      "localKey": "MC-2",
      "language": "en",
      "refs": [
        "MC-2"
      ],
      "span": {
        "file": "docs/curriculum/blueprints/math.prob.law-of-unconscious.md",
        "startLine": 29,
        "endLine": 29
      }
    },
    {
      "assetId": "math.prob.law-of-unconscious/misconception/MC-3",
      "kind": "misconception",
      "conceptId": "math.prob.law-of-unconscious",
      "title": "LOTUS-ONLY-WORKS-FOR-MONOTONE-g",
      "content": "| MC-3 | LOTUS-ONLY-WORKS-FOR-MONOTONE-g | Student thinks LOTUS requires g to be invertible/monotone; confuses LOTUS with the change-of-variable theorem for densities | Type 5 — instruction-induced (the change-of-variable theorem for f_Y DOES require monotonicity (or piecewise monotonicity); LOTUS for E[g(X)] does not — it works for any measurable g) |",
      "localKey": "MC-3",
      "language": "en",
      "refs": [
        "MC-3"
      ],
      "span": {
        "file": "docs/curriculum/blueprints/math.prob.law-of-unconscious.md",
        "startLine": 30,
        "endLine": 30
      }
    },
